Gated Graph Neural Networks for Learning Hidden Independent Cascade Dynamics Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.04033v1 [cs.SI] 02 Oct 2026 Gated Graph Neural Networks for Learning Hidden Independent Cascade Dynamics Anubha Goel Illia Oleksiienko Mateusz Wilinski Alexandros Iosifidis Juho Kanniainen Abstract Information and infectious diseases spread through social networks, but the spreading probabilities driving them are hard to estimate without per-node activation times. Applications seldom supply these, and inference must instead proceed through indirect and noisy proxies for the terminal infection states. We study this inverse problem on a fixed, known graph under the Hidden Independent Cascade (HIC) model, with one spreading probability per node rather than a single global rate, so the number of unknowns scales with the number of nodes. Seed sets and observation parameters are known, while activation times and terminal infection states are latent, and the observed-data likelihood requires marginalizing over every spreading outcome. We propose a simulation-based amortized estimator that recovers the full node-level parameter vector without reconstructing individual latent cascades. Repeated seed-conditioned symptom observations are summarized as Symptom-Aware Cascade Features (SACF), which combine empirical symptom statistics with neighborhood and structural information. SACF are mapped to parameters by SAGE-HC, a permutation-equivariant gated graph neural network whose learned gates attenuate neighborhood messages corrupted by false positives and false negatives, and training on simulated HIC realizations yields a reusable inverse map. As a benchmark under the same hidden observations, we extend the Dynamic Message Passing learning framework to the HIC emission model. On synthetic and empirical graphs the two methods separate by topology. DMP is highly accurate on trees, whereas SAGE-HC is substantially better on heterogeneous, loopy graphs under noisy terminal symptoms. 1 Introduction Network models in general, and spreading dynamics on networks in particular, constitute a useful family of tools for modeling problems in epidemiology ( Pastor-Satorras et al., 2015 ) , finance ( Acemoglu et al., 2015 ) , information spreading ( Zapperi et al., 2025 ) , and many other fields ( Newman, 2018 ; Barrat et al., 2008 ) . Many widely used spreading models have only a small number of node states, discrete-time evolution, and simple local stochastic rules. One of the advantages of such models is their simplicity, which often permits direct computations and efficient Monte Carlo simulations. Nevertheless, inverse tasks for spreading processes, like recovering the parameters of the dynamics from observations, can remain computationally demanding, especially when the spreading process is only indirectly observed. In this work, we aim to estimate a separate spreading probability for every node from indirect observations made only after the cascade has terminated. The underlying dynamics follow the Independent Cascade model on a fixed and known directed graph, with one parameter per node shared across its outgoing edges, so the number of unknowns equals the number of nodes. The data record not the cascades themselves but what they emit, namely a known seed set and a noisy terminal symptom vector for each of a set of independent cascades. Neither the per-node activation times used by standard cascade-inference methods nor the terminal infection field is available. The parameters of the symptom-observation mechanism are assumed known, so the spreading parameters are the sole target of estimation. These two sources of information loss are of different kinds. The first is temporal. Observations arrive only after the cascade has terminated, so there is no ordering to induce the increasing sequence of activated subgraphs that standard cascade data provide. The second concerns the state itself. Because the terminal infection field is seen only through noisy symptoms, a positively symptomatic node need not be connected to the seeds through a path of live edges, and an infected node may produce no symptom at all. The observations therefore contain both false positives and false negatives. Direct likelihood evaluation requires marginalizing over a large number of latent spreading outcomes, separately for every cascade, and the resulting objective must be optimized over as many unknowns as there are nodes. In practice, complete cascade data can rarely be assumed. Our setting therefore arises naturally in applications where the underlying spreading state is latent and only noisy proxies are observed. Examples include financial markets, where private information diffusion is inferred from trading behavior or investor proximity ( Baltakys et al., 2019 ; Baltakys et al., 2023 ; Goel et al., 2025 ) ; epidemiology, where infections are latent and symptoms provide imperfect signals ( Braunstein and Ingrosso, 2016 ; Allen et al., 2020 ) ; cybersecurity, where alerts act as proxies for compromised systems ( Munoz-Gonzalez et al., 2017 ; Kazeminajafabadi and Imani, 2023 ) ; and online information diffusion, where latent adoption is observed through engagement or resharing behavior ( Bakshy et al., 2009 ; Taylor et al., 2013 ) . Early work on inverse problems for spreading processes focused on fully observable cascades and showed how maximum-likelihood methods can be used to learn model parameters or reconstruct the underlying network ( Saito et al., 2008 ; Gomez-Rodriguez et al., 2012 ; Pouget-Abadie and Horel, 2015 ) . In many applications, however, activation times are not recorded at all, and learning must rely on the final outcome of each cascade ( Amin et al., 2014 ) , where guarantees for recovering the network hold for tree-like graphs but weaken as short cycles become common. The observations may also be incomplete, with some nodes unobserved throughout, and they may themselves be noisy. Direct maximum-likelihood inference then becomes computationally expensive because it requires summation over the unobserved part of the system ( Lokhov, 2016 ) . One way to approximate this computation is to use Dynamic Message Passing (DMP) as a subroutine for learning ( Wilinski and Lokhov, 2021 ) . The resulting Scalable Learning of Independent Cascade Effective Representation framework was shown to handle several forms of noise and incompleteness in cascade data ( Wilinski and Lokhov, 2024 ) , and provides the natural likelihood-based comparison for learning spreading parameters from noisy and partially observed data. More generally, message passing is a widely used approximation for both forward and inverse inference in network dynamics ( Karrer and Newman, 2010 ; Altarelli et al., 2014 ; Li and Saad, 2021 ; Crotti and Braunstein, 2023 ) , including reconstruction tasks relevant to our work ( Braunstein et al., 2019 ) . Several other methods have addressed inference from incomplete cascade observations. In ( Dutta et al., 2018 ) , likelihood-free approximate Bayesian computation (ABC) is used to jointly infer a small set of spreading parameters and the source from multiple fully observed snapshots. Extending this line of work, Wang and Onnela (2024) used neural-network-compressed ABC to learn informative summary statistics and infer spreading parameters on a known network from a sequence of positive and negative tests, without observing infection and removal times. An autoregressive neural-network method within a Bayesian framework was proposed in ( Biazzo et al., 2022 ) , but its computational cost restricts its applicability to relatively small networks. Independent Cascade network inference from probabilistic terminal infection statuses was studied in ( Huang et al., 2024 ) , but focused on reconstructing unknown influence relationships rather than estimating node-level spreading parameters on a known graph. More recently, Manoharan et al. (2025) introduced the Hidden Independent Cascade (HIC) model and proposed a likelihood-free procedure in which a classifier is trained to distinguish observed cascades from simulated ones, while the model parameters are adjusted until the two become indistinguishable. Although effective for low-dimensional parameterizations, this approach does not scale effectively to the heterogeneous setting considered here, in which the number of unknown parameters grows with the number of nodes. We retain the HIC generative mechanism introduced in ( Manoharan et al., 2025 ) , but instantiate its spreading layer with a heterogeneous node parameterization. In contrast to the low-dimensional likelihood-free estimation considered there, our goal is to infer a separate spreading probability for every node from repeated observations of known seeds and noisy terminal symptoms. We address this problem using a graph-based estimator trained on simulated HIC realizations, which shares information across nodes through the graph rather than treating each parameter on its own. Our main contributions are as follows. First, we provide a formal mathematical formulation of the heterogeneous HIC inverse problem under noisy terminal observations, including the latent spreading process, observation mechanism, and observed-data likelihood. Second, we introduce Symptom-Aware Cascade Features (SACF) , which aggregate repeated hidden-cascade observations across cascades and seed configurations into structured node-level summaries. SACF combine symptom frequencies and variability with neighborhood co-activation, graph-distance, and self-seeded spreading information, without reconstructing individual latent cascades. Third, we propose the Symptom-Aware Gated Estimator for Hidden Cascades (SAGE-HC) , a permutation-equivariant graph neural network based on gated message passing. Training SAGE-HC on simulated spreading-parameter assignments and corresponding HIC observations yields a form of simulation-based amortized inference ( Zammit-Mangion et al., 2025 ) , learning a reusable inverse map from graph-aware cascade summaries to node-level spreading parameters. Fourth, we extend the Dynamic Message Passing learning framework of ( Wilinski and Lokhov, 2021 ; Wilinski and Lokhov, 2024 ) to the HIC observation model, providing a likelihood-based benchmark under the same hidden terminal observations. Finally, we systematically examine the effects of network topology, observation noise, training data, cascade budget, repeated feature realizations, and test-time observation budgets on estimation accuracy and stability. 2 Model and Method Our approach consists of three stages. We first specify the HIC spreading and observation model, then construct SACF from the observed cascades, and finally use SAGE-HC to estimate the node-level spreading parameters. 2.1 Hidden Independent Cascade: Problem Formulation The Independent Cascade (IC) model is a standard probabilistic model of spreading on networks, in which activated nodes attempt to activate each neighbor once, independently, according to fixed transmission probabilities. In many applications, however, activations are not directly observed; instead, only noisy terminal signals (e.g., symptoms, test results, or behavioral traces) are available as imperfect proxies for infection. The HIC model extends IC with an explicit observation layer: the spreading process follows IC dynamics, while the terminal infection state remains latent and only noisy emissions are observed. Let ( Ω , ℱ , ℙ Θ ) (\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P}_{\Theta}) be a probability space supporting the spreading and observation processes, where Θ \Theta indexes the induced law ℙ Θ \mathbb{P}_{\Theta} and comprises the diffusion and observation parameters. Let G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) be a finite directed graph w